WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:03.070
Everyone goes to the movies for different
reasons.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.780
Some people like just pure escapism, while
others want to be intellectually stimulated,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.380
or have a good laugh.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.750
However, nobody goes to the movies because
they just want to be totally confused, which

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.990
is a major problem when it comes to these
six movies that still don't make any sense

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:17.820
to people.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.280
...UNTIL NOW!

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.020
Trance

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:25.180
This Danny Boyle picture didn't make a huge
impression on moviegoers when it came out,

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.850
with a twisty heist plot one reviewer noted
as "purposely convoluted" and "impossible

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:30.850
to keep up with."

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.660
It doesn't help that the movie plays fast
and loose with the bounds of reality, particularly

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.190
with the concept of therapeutic hypnosis,
a real thing that the film portrays as something

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.190
closer to sorcery than science.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:45.800
But it turns out that under all that intentional
confusion, the film's plot is actually really

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:46.800
straightforward.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.210
Elizabeth, the therapist played by Rosario
Dawson, was behind the art heist central to

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.210
the movie.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.789
See, she once was in an ill-fated relationship
with Simon, played by James McAvoy.

00:00:56.789 --> 00:01:00.980
In using hypnosis to make Simon forget their
relationship, she also implanted the idea

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.559
of an art heist in his mind.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:07.020
During the attempt to recall Simon's memories,
Elizabeth forms a brief relationship with

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.359
Vincent Cassel's criminal boss Franck, but
he ends up as manipulated as everyone else

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:16.159
by Elizabeth, an unbelievably smart puppetmaster
who was willing to let a lot of people die

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:17.679
to get her hands on some money.

00:01:17.679 --> 00:01:19.729
It all makes sense now.

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:21.369
Uh.. right?

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:23.409
Synecdoche, New York

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:29.240
It's no exaggeration to say that 2008's Synecdoche,
New York is a movie about everything and everyone.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.289
As Roger Ebert noted in his laudatory review,
it's a story about "you, whoever you are."

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:38.389
The sprawling, ever-changing cast, which features
different actors playing the same characters

00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:42.609
in the production over the course of decades,
speaks to the essence of identity, how we're

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:46.709
seen and perceived as we perform our roles
in life, with our small successes, painful

00:01:46.709 --> 00:01:48.429
failures, and major flaws.

00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:53.140
Caden Cotard, played by Philip Seymour Hoffman,
is an artist obsessed with creating the perfect

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:57.249
artwork, and accomplishing the goal is just
as impossible as living a perfect life.

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:59.520
No one can do it, and we will all die trying.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.270
The key to understanding works by Charlie
Kaufman, the scribe behind cinematic mind-benders

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:09.069
such as Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless Mind
and Being John Malkovich, is that there's

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:12.120
no distinction between the dream world and
reality.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.690
Everything is real, existing together in a
surrealistic stew, and the impossible aspects

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.890
of the movie represent real feelings.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:22.909
Very little in his films are literal, but
everything feels true.

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:24.140
Solaris

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.959
In Steven Soderbergh's 2002 mindbender Solaris,
a space station hovers over a mysterious ocean

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:34.329
planet, which despite decades of research
has remained unknowable to its observers.

00:02:34.329 --> 00:02:38.980
Worse, the only data scientists on Earth have
received from the station has been borderline

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.959
nonsensical, and eyewitness reports of impossible
beings on the planet's surface from people

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:45.879
who've been there are dismissed as hallucinations.

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:50.150
Chris, a psychologist from Earth, makes the
journey to Solaris to examine the state of

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.050
the crew, and decide if observations should
continue.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:58.069
But upon arrival, he too begins to experience
impossible phenomena, including visitations

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:01.370
from the phantom of his long-dead wife, somehow
miraculously revived.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.340
At the end of the film, Chris is visited by
the apparition of a child.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.110
When he takes the child's hand, Chris seems
to suddenly be back at home on Earth, with

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.769
his wife alive beside him.

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:13.950
Only, then we see the surface of Solaris and
the film ends.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.469
So...what the heck happened?

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:17.610
Did he die and become one of the planet's
phantasms?

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.450
Is he alive on Solaris, living in a fantasy
world?

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.840
Did he actually get sent home somehow?

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.769
It's up to you as a viewer to decide.

00:03:24.769 --> 00:03:28.620
"Do you understand what I'm trying to tell
you?

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.580
There are no answers, only choices."

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.180
Demon

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:39.160
This highly-regarded 2015 Polish horror picture
follows an English groom as he arrives in

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.939
his fiancée's native Poland for a grand wedding
before her gathered family.

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:47.510
After an encounter in the yard with a buried
skeleton, the protagonist's emotional state,

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.330
and his sanity, starts to fray.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:53.370
He's haunted by visions and possessed by a
dead woman who wants to live and love again,

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.859
her takeover of his body culminating in his
utter breakdown as the family presses on with

00:03:57.859 --> 00:03:58.859
the ceremony.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.079
Near the end, one family member mentions that
the husband is gone, and indeed, he never

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:06.150
appears again, and the movie ends quietly
soon after.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.319
But what does this mean?

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:08.920
Did he vanish of his own accord?

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.080
Was he taken by the demon?

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.930
Or did the wife's family do him in to be rid
of him?

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.959
There are no answers in this anticlimax.

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:18.680
The family's response, though, is clear: raze
over the wedding site.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.729
Forget this ever happened.

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:22.220
Put his truck in a lake and pretend he was
never here.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:27.120
The film seems to be a metaphor for postwar
wounds and the distrust sowed by nationalism:

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.930
you can bury the past all you want, but your
history will always find a way to resurface.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.080
It just… might not make any sense when it
does.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.840
Upstream Color

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.949
If you were confused by the critically acclaimed
2013 indie film Upstream Color, don't worry,

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:43.639
because we're going to explain it as simply
as possible.

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:47.110
Kris is infected by a parasite that plugs
her into a shared consciousness, putting her

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.400
in a suggestible state that leads her to destroy
her life.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.470
Drawn to a pig farmer who uses music to attract
the parasite, Kris has the parasite removed.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.510
It is then placed in pigs, who are chucked
into the river to drown.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:03.260
After they die, the parasites infect orchids,
leading to larvae, which are harvested to

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.970
create new parasites, which are used to infect
more people and expand the shared emotional

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:09.700
network even further.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:13.970
Kris and Jeff, another former host, interrupt
this process by killing the farmer.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:18.110
They then gather the rest of the victims at
the farm and form a new community, breaking

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.620
the cycle of trauma.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.780
The film is essentially an extended metaphor
for human emotional connectivity.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.080
Simple, see?

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.080
Arrival

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.810
At the end of the acclaimed 2016 sci-fi drama
Arrival, alien visitors patiently explain

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.020
the whole movie to their human interpreter,
Louise, played by Amy Adams.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.460
Despite this, though, many fans left the theater
still scratching their heads.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:40.460
Why?

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.910
Well, it turned out those flashbacks showing
the loss of Louise's young daughter weren't

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.530
flashbacks at all, but visions of Louise's
tragic future.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.350
The mind-blowing twist was confusing enough
for some fans that a large portion of the

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:55.890
internet was devoted to explaining it, a process
that, clearly, is still ongoing.

